From: David J. Vandenburgh <david@vandenburgh.co>
Subject: [PATCH] input: rsinput: accept large UART batches instead of dropping them

A serdev callback larger than the receive buffer was thrown away whole,
and a callback that didn't fit behind a buffered partial frame threw the
partial frame away. Either way good reports were lost, and after a stall
(resume, a busy CPU) the backlog arrives in exactly such large batches.

Take only as many bytes as fit and return that count: serdev keeps the
rest and offers it again after the complete frames have been drained.
The parser never leaves the buffer full, so there is always room.

Adapted from Armada's 0515a (accept large UART batches) to the parser
1065 introduced.

Link: https://github.com/armada-os/armada/blob/b58df0a1ae086da5f39ef3647f63db5a2288f82f/packages/kernel/patches/0515a-input-rsinput-accept-large-uart-batches.patch
---
--- a/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
+++ b/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
@@ -500,17 +500,14 @@
         return count;
     }
 
-    if (count > sizeof(drv->rx_buf)) {
-        dev_warn_ratelimited(&serdev->dev, "RX buffer overflow\n");
-        return count;
-    }
-
     /*
-     * If the buffered partial frame plus the new bytes would overflow the
-     * buffer, discard the partial frame and resynchronise on the new bytes.
+     * Take only what fits and leave the rest with serdev, which offers it
+     * again once the complete frames below have been drained. The parser
+     * never leaves a full buffer behind (a frame is at most the buffer's
+     * size and is consumed as soon as it is complete), so there is always
+     * room for at least one byte.
      */
-    if (drv->rx_len + count > sizeof(drv->rx_buf))
-        drv->rx_len = 0;
+    count = min(count, sizeof(drv->rx_buf) - drv->rx_len);
 
     memcpy(drv->rx_buf + drv->rx_len, data, count);
     drv->rx_len += count;
